From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Mon, 3 Oct 1994 12:34:00 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-10-33] Non-destructive upload?

aberenzw@minerva.cis.yale.edu (_madAdam_) wrote:

>So, you suggest that picking a brain a part to determine its structure
>would actually be done by _physically_ picking the brain apart?  OK... if
>you say so!  I dare to offer an alternative to this graceful procedure: if
>structural analysis of the brain, neuron by neruon, is what it takes, then
>that can be accomplished with advanced electromagnetic imaging techniques.

As far as I know, most plausible methods are rather irreversible.
Since the brain is such a complex system with parts hidden inside,
getting at the fine structure is not easy. 

Using electromagnetic imaging has certain drawbacks. According to
calculations made on the cryonics mailing list a few years back,
the necessary energy densities would vaporise the brain. Since
synaptic structures are so small, high frequencies would be
needed. But the brain doesn't absorb them very well, so the energy
has to be rather intense. So electromagnetic scans are also
destructive (and rather spectacular; I love the image of using
gamma-ray lasers driven by atomic explosions in space to image
brains onto holographic film moving at relativistic speeds). 

A more passive scan, studying the electrical impulses is also
probably rather infeasible. Even if we could get the necessary
resolution (using nanotechnological SQUIDS?), we would only see
the electromagnetic interactions, and miss the chemical and
biological structures. 


>However, I hestitate to say that this would be necessary; nanobots could
>explore and map out the mess inside my skull, tracing the path of each
>neuron. ( how many nanobots would it take to perform such a task in
>reasonable time? )

Probably a lot, but thats not a problem if we got assemblers. I
think a successive "colonization" method would work, where the
nanomachines set up bases inside decoy glia-cells (to get workroom
and protection) and then range out into the neigbouring brain,
setting up new camps. I would probably try to successively replace
the cells with nanotechnology instead, but just non-destructively
measuring the cells might be possible too. 

However, there are many problems (as I described on
rec.arts.sf.science earlier). The nanosystems need "food", so
neither they have to live of standard biochemicals or the patient
has to eat very odd nutrient supplements. They have to avoid
triggering immunological responses (very nasty if that happens in
the brain). And then there is the problem with heat. We cannot
have the nanomachines run at their maximum speed unless we have
enough cooling (and we certainly don't want to boil the brain), so
they must act rather slowly. 

I have no real estimates of the numbers involved, but there are
around 10^11 neurons in the brain, and each has between 10^2 and
10^3 synapses. We will probably need many machines just for each
synapse, so lets say we need 100 machines to study, store and
possibly replace each synapse.  That would imply around 10^4-10^5
machines just for the synapses of a single cell. Then we need
other machines to map out each cell, but maybe we can reuse the
synapse-measurers for this. So a very rough estimate would be on
the order of 10^16 machines to upload the whole brain, but of
course we can reuse many of them. 


>I think its safe to say that no one is going to be dissecting _my_ noggin...

Its better having your brain dissected than being dead...

