From: "John M. Bozeman" <jmb5b@faraday.clas.virginia.edu>
Date: Mon, 3 Oct 1994 19:10:29 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-10-37] Non-destructive upload?

I think another possible way to increase the information available
to NMR/MRI is through the use of radioactive tags...  As I recall
from my organic chem classes of long ago, certain nuclei resonate
and others don't, depending upon the the isotope of the element; 
a  carbon NMR, for example, sees C-13 but not the more common C-12.
Perhaps spiking a person's diet with various radiochemicals could help
increase resolution, particularly in the cases of trace elements.
However, it might also work in the case of some of the more common ones;
a substancial amount of the body's hydrogen could be replaced with
deuterium, for example.   (I'm not sure if this particular isotope
"spike" would help, as I am not sure whether it is H-1 or H-2 that
resonates.)

