From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Mon, 3 Oct 94 21:29:26 WET DST
Subject: [#94-10-40] "Proof" in math

EdRegis@aol.com writes:
> question from the audience (paraphrasing): "It's a known fact that any
> computer program has a hundred or so bugs in it, so does a computer
> simulation really constitute a proof of a theorem?"  
> 
> Thurston's answer (paraphasing): "Yeah.  Traditional deductive proofs in
> mathematics probably have far more bugs in them than any program does.
>  Nobody reads such proofs all the way through, anyway.  Mathematicians like
> to know the details are there, but nobody really checks them."  
> 
> First I heard of *that* viewpoint!    


   I think he was pulling the student's leg. Look how fast the subtle
flaw in Wiles's fermat theorem proof was found. Speaking as a math
major, mathematicians check details of proofs, however, they don't
reprove any theorems used in the theorem. i.e. If here is a famous
"result" used in the theorem, a mathematician need only check that
it was applied correctly.

   On the other hand, math is getting so complex nowadays that
proofs span hundreds of pages and/or computer assisted, so it wouldn't
surprise me if this is becoming a common error. I believe the
proof of the classification of all simple groups in abstract algebra
was 1000+ pages. The proof of the four color theorem probably spanned
ten times that in computer printouts.

   Math will probably be one of the first fields to be "roboticized"
Genetic Algorithms + Genetic Programming + Expert Systems + theorem
provers = mathematician out of a job. Of course, then the art of
mathematics will shift to higher levels of abstraction, refinement
of automated proof finders, and metamathematical proof of the
theorem provers/finders themselves.


-Ray

